WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.360
Okay, so I've had a lot of requests for a video 
on the North Germanic languages.

00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:09.719
So today, I present to you a video 
on Swedish, Norwegian, Danish and Finnish.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.019
[Crowd protestation]

00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:12.699
What?

00:00:12.699 --> 00:00:14.839
The North Germanic languages 
of the Nordic Nations

00:00:14.839 --> 00:00:16.739
And Finnish isn't one of them.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.559
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel 
and my name is Paul

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.080
Today, I'm going to talk about the North Germanic 
languages of the Nordic Nations.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.420
That includes the scandinavian languages: 
Swedish, Danish and Norwegian.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.079
And I will also touch on the languages of 
Icelandic and Faroese.

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:36.039
I wanted to call this video:
the Scandinavian Languages

00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:38.859
because I just love the way that word sounds: 
"Scandinavian"

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.700
But to the people of that region, the word "Scandinavian" 
only refers to Denmark, Sweden and Norway

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.359
and not to the other countries that I want to talk about.

00:00:47.439 --> 00:00:53.119
So if I say the Nordic Nations, that also includes 
Iceland, the Faroe Islands as well as Finland.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.160
I want to talk about the North Germanic languages 
spoken in all of those countries.

00:00:57.179 --> 00:01:01.380
So I decided on the title: 
the North Germanic languages of the Nordic Nations.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.780
And please note that Finnish is not 
a North Germanic language.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.719
It belongs to a separate language family entirely.

00:01:07.959 --> 00:01:10.259
But there is a Swedish-speaking miniority in Finland.

00:01:10.379 --> 00:01:13.739
There are about 20 million native speakers 
of North Germanic languages.

00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:16.818
And that includes about 9 million speakers of Swedish,

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:20.319
mainly in Sweden but also 
as a minority language in Finland.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.040
Six million speakers of Danish, mainly in Denmark

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.438
but also as a minority language in the "Southern Schleswig" 
region of Northern Germany and in Greenland.

00:01:28.438 --> 00:01:31.158
5 million speakers of Norwegian, mainly in Norway

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.079
320,000 speakers of Icelandic, mainly in Iceland

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:41.099
90,000 speakers of Faroese, about 2/3 of them living 
in the Faroe Islands and the rest mainly in Denmark

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:46.459
The North Germanic languages are, as you probably guessed, 
a branch of the Germanic language family.

00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:52.060
All Germanic languages developed from Proto-Germanic,
which was spoken around 500 BCE

00:01:52.359 --> 00:01:55.400
Proto-Germanic possibly originated in Scandinavia

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.200
and different varieties of Germanic
began to emerge with migration.

00:01:59.239 --> 00:02:08.638
Runic inscriptions from the 2nd century CE show us that, 
by that time, Proto-Germanic had began to separate 
into distinct Western, Eastern and Northern dialects.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:14.420
The northern dialect was spoken in Scandinavia
and is today often referred to as Proto-Norse.

00:02:14.438 --> 00:02:17.500
And became the ancestor 
of all the North Germanic languages.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.259
Proto-Norse was spoken from
around the 2nd century CE to the 8th century CE

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.819
and, by the beginning of the Viking Era
 in the eighth century CE,

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.200
It had evolved into the dialects that are 
collectively referred to as "Old Norse".

00:02:30.659 --> 00:02:36.280
During the next few hundred years, Vikings, seafaring 
norse people, explored much of europe by sea and river,

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:40.379
conquering lands and establishing settlements
and bringing their language with them.

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:45.359
During this time, "Old Norse" was divided 
into three mutually intelligible dialects of

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.780
"Old East Norse", "Old West Norse" and "Old Gutnish"

00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:56.318
"Old East Norse" was spoken in Sweden and Denmark, 
as well as their overseas settlements in Russia,
England and in Danish settlements in Normandy.

00:02:56.659 --> 00:03:03.219
"Old West Norse" was spoken in Norway as well 
as its overseas settlements, the two most notable 
were Iceland and the Faroe Islands.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:08.340
But also Ireland, Scotland and the Isle of Man
and Norwegian settlements in Normandy.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:14.979
Old Gutnish was mainly spoken on the island of Gotland, 
which is today part of Sweden as well as 
some overseas settlements to the East.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:23.120
These West, East and Gutnish varieties of Old Norse 
gradually developed into the modern North Germanic 
languages around the 14th century CE.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.759
The western branch of languages consists of 
Norwegian, Icelandic and Faroese,

00:03:27.879 --> 00:03:29.859
as well as some other extinct languages.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.240
The eastern branch consists of Swedish and Danish.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:38.920
The Gutnish branch consists of only
the Gutnish language,  which is still spoken 
to some extent on the island of Gotland.

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:44.218
But these days, the North Germanic languages are generally 
not thought of in terms of East, West and Gutnish.

00:03:44.218 --> 00:03:47.198
They are thought of in terms of continental and insular.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.658
The Continental languages are 
Danish, Swedish and Norwegian,

00:03:50.658 --> 00:03:53.558
and the insular languages are Icelandic and Faroese.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.180
These categories are based 
on the mutual intelligibility of the languages,

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.360
rather than on the genetic root language 
that they come from.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:07.560
Norwegian is grouped with Swedish and Danish because,
even though Icelandic and Norwegian developed 
from the same Old West Norse,

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.219
Norwegian is today much more intelligible 
with Swedish and Danish.

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:18.019
One reason for that is the political union of 
Denmark and Norway from 1536 to 1814.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:23.620
During this time, the written Norwegian language 
stopped being used and it was replaced 
by the written Danish language

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:29.660
and this had a big impact on the spoken dialects of
Norwegian, especially the central and eastern dialects.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:34.180
The Continental languages also underwent 
a lot of influence from Middle Low German,

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:37.579
which is an influence that didn't affect
Icelandic and Faroese.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.139
The three continental languages can be referred to 
as the "Scandinavian languages".

00:04:42.259 --> 00:04:49.439
When talking about the Scandinavian languages, 
it's important to note that there is a significant amount 
of dialectal variation within each language.

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:52.860
In fact, the three languages are made up 
of a dialect continuum,

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.699
that means if you travel in one direction, 
the dialects gradually change the further you go.

00:04:57.699 --> 00:05:01.439
That means for example that, if you are Norwegian living 
near the border with Sweden,

00:05:01.439 --> 00:05:05.619
you probably have an easier time understanding 
your neighbor just across the border in Sweden

00:05:05.619 --> 00:05:08.619
than you do a Norwegian 
from the other side of the country,

00:05:08.839 --> 00:05:12.859
even though you are supposedly speaking 
a different language from that Swedish person.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:17.180
This makes it kind of hard to determine at what point 
these dialects become different languages.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:19.319
Or if they are in fact different languages at all.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:25.000
The three languages are all more or less intelligible,
depending on where the dialect lies on that continuum.

00:05:25.139 --> 00:05:28.139
And the written languages are almost entirely intelligible.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:34.460
Danish seems to be the odd man out,
with its complex phonology that has come 
to be quite distinct from the written language.

00:05:34.839 --> 00:05:39.638
Swedes and Norwegians often say that Danish people 
sound like they're speaking with a potato in their mouth.

00:05:39.959 --> 00:05:43.019
I can't actually confirm if they do speak 
with the potato in their mouth.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:46.639
So, Scandinavians, can you let me know 
if that's true in the comments down below?

00:05:46.839 --> 00:05:49.198
I have Danish roots. 
So I'm allowed to make fun of Danes.

00:05:49.319 --> 00:05:54.399
From what I understand, Norwegians have the easiest 
time understanding the other two Scandinavian languages.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.120
Though, they understand Swedish better than Danish.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:03.420
Swedes can generally understand Norwegian 
but they have much more trouble understanding Danish, 
because of its pronunciation

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:08.920
And Danes can more or less understand Norwegian and, 
to a lesser extent, they can understand some Swedish.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.600
From what I understand, Scandinavians will 
generally not speak the other person's language.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.278
They will speak their own language, while making 
an effort to understand the other person's speech.

00:06:18.459 --> 00:06:21.539
And maybe, they will slow down 
and clarify things when necessary.

00:06:21.939 --> 00:06:25.920
But, when they have significant trouble
communicating, they might switch to English,

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:30.800
which isn't that tough for them because Scandinavians 
are magical geniuses when it comes to learning English.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:36.560
In this kind of situation where you have three closely related
languages that kind of blend together on a continuum,

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:43.819
the languages are not defined by the spoken variety, 
but rather by the official standard language associated 
with that country or region.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:50.180
In Denmark, there is Standard Danish.
In Sweden, there is Standard Swedish.
And, in Norway, there is Standard Norwegian.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:53.459
Or wait, no, there are actually two Standard Norwegians.

00:06:53.459 --> 00:06:54.899
What?

00:06:54.939 --> 00:06:58.699
That's right, Norway actually has 
two official standard written languages:

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.460
Bokmål and Nynorsk.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:04.920
Bokmål which means "book language", 
is a Danish-influenced standard language,

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.980
which is very close to standard Danish
but which uses Norwegian pronunciation.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:17.240
Nynorsk or "new Norwegian" is intended to be 
a purer version of Norwegian, based on
Norway's more conservative Western dialects

00:07:17.819 --> 00:07:22.019
Which standard language Norwegians are educated in 
depends on the region they grow up in.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.639
But, despite having two official standard languages, 
Norwegians don't really speak them.

00:07:26.699 --> 00:07:30.659
Norwegians generally speak their own local dialect, 
whenever they are speaking.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:35.579
Even in formal situations and even when they're speaking 
to people from the other scandinavian countries.

00:07:35.579 --> 00:07:40.439
Because of this, Norwegians have to get used to
understanding a wide variety of spoken dialects

00:07:40.439 --> 00:07:44.680
And that's probably part of what makes it easier 
for them to understand Swedish and Danish.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.660
But that, of course, is just my speculation. 
So, native speakers, you can confirm or disconfirm that.

00:07:49.839 --> 00:07:54.199
So let's take a look at the three continental languages 
and see just how similar they are.

