The end of the second word seems to vanish.

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:47.340
This could be part of 
that "potato phenomenon" discussed earlier.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:50.740
So you can probably see just how similar 
these three languages are.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:55.440
And you can probably see how - relatively speaking - 
they are easy for English-speakers to learn.

00:10:55.620 --> 00:11:03.020
They are all category 1 languages, according to 
the American Foreign Service Institute, which trains 
diplomats for their overseas assignments.

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:07.260
None of the North Germanic languages are amongst
the most widely spoken languages in the world.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:11.060
And people from the Nordic countries
generally speak excellent English.

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:14.760
So, is it a waste of time to learn 
a North Germanic language? Of course not!

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.900
If you're interested in the cultures 
and the history of that region,

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:20.800
then learning one of the languages
could bring you immense joy.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:28.560
And because the three Scandinavian languages are 
so similar, learning one of them can unlock the doors 
to the other ones, especially of the written languages.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:32.140
And, if you're an avid traveler 
or you want to go backpacking around the world,

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:35.280
then you will probably meet 
a surprising number of Scandinavians.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:39.480
And, knowing their language or even one 
of the other two languages will help you break the ice

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:42.980
and get to know some wonderful 
and, possibly, highly attractive people.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:47.860
And if you are interested in the old Nordic cultures, 
then you might benefit from learning Icelandic.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:51.160
Since written Icelandic is still very similar to Old Norse.

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.580
So don't hesitate to start learning 
one of the North Germanic languages,

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:57.580
which could be your portal into a whole new world

00:11:57.800 --> 00:11:59.100
So the question of the day:

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:04.520
Native speakers of North Germanic languages,
what is your experience communicating 
with the speakers of other languages?

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.080
Which of the other languages 
do you understand the most?

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:10.220
And how do you bridge that communication gap?
We'd like to know.

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:13.200
And everyone else, jump in 
and leave whatever comment you want.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:16.420
Thank you for watching and have a nice day.

